A topology on a set X is a family τ of subsets of X (called open sets) satisfying:
1. Both ∅ and X belong to τ;
2. Arbitrary unions of sets in τ belong to τ: if {U_{i}}_{i∈I} ⊆ τ, then ⋃_{i∈I} U_{i} ∈ τ;
3. Finite intersections of sets in τ belong to τ: if {V_{j}}^{n}_{j=1} ⊆ τ, then ⋂^{n}_{j=1} V_{j} ∈ τ.
The pair (X, τ) is called a topological space.

Definition 1.10. A relation on a set S is any subset R ⊆ S × S. If (a, b) ∈ R,
we say that a is related to b and write aRb.
   A relation R is called
   • reflexive if aRa for all a ∈ S;
   • symmetric if aRb implies bRa;
   • transitive if aRb and bRc imply aRc;
   • antisymmetric if aRb and bRa imply a = b.

